{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"caller":{"type":"direct"},"id":"toolu_01KBc68t9YesAoWi9kLL9g8u","input":{"questions":[{"header":"Read path","multiSelect":false,"options":[{"description":"Spawn `opencode export --pure <id>`. Keeps cledger's zero-runtime-deps and Node>=20 engines intact, insulates us from DB schema churn (that empty session_message table), and never touches the credential/token tables. Cost: requires `opencode` on PATH, and re-exports the whole session each capture (a cursor still makes appends incremental).","label":"`opencode export` CLI (Recommended)"},{"description":"Use node:sqlite. No subprocess, true incremental queries. Cost: node:sqlite needs Node >=22.5 so package.json engines must move off >=20; we'd be reverse-engineering a schema that is visibly mid-migration; and we'd be opening a DB that also stores API tokens.","label":"Read SQLite directly"},{"description":"Try the CLI, drop to direct DB reads when opencode isn't on PATH. Most robust, roughly double the code and test surface, and still pays the engines bump.","label":"Export CLI, fall back to SQLite"}],"question":"How should the adapter read opencode's data?"},{"header":"Trigger","multiSelect":false,"options":[{"description":"`cledger install opencode` writes ~/.config/opencode/plugin/cledger.js listening for `session.idle`, which spawns `cledger hook opencode`. Per-turn capture matching claude-code/codex. Cost: we now ship and maintain third-party JS; the hook must use `--pure` so re-entering opencode doesn't reload our own plugin.","label":"Install an opencode plugin (Recommended)"},{"description":"No plugin. `cledger capture opencode --session <id>` plus a `--all` that sweeps every session for the current repo via `opencode session list`. Far simpler and nothing to install, but capture only happens when you remember (or at commit/push time).","label":"Manual / sweep only"},{"description":"Ship the sweep command first as the reliable floor, add the plugin for per-turn capture. Most work, but the sweep also serves backfill and covers sessions that predate the install.","label":"Both: plugin plus a sweep command"}],"question":"What triggers capture?"},{"header":"Granularity","multiSelect":false,"options":[{"description":"Codex-like. A part is small and immutable once complete, so incremental capture is idempotent at the finest available unit. Critically: if a message gains parts after an early session.idle, already-captured parts are untouched and only new parts append. Per-message would instead re-emit changed content under a new id — a duplicate.","label":"One event per part (Recommended)"},{"description":"claude-code-like: parts become the blocks array, one turn per visible agent turn. Cleanest for consumers like intent-recall. But an opencode message spans many LLM steps, so events get large, and any part appended to an already-captured message duplicates it.","label":"One event per message"},{"description":"Per-message granularity, gated on time.completed being set. Reduces but does not eliminate the duplicate risk, and drops content from sessions abandoned mid-turn.","label":"Per message, but only completed ones"}],"question":"opencode's store is mutable, so a positional seq can shift. What granularity should an event be?"},{"header":"Subagents","multiSelect":false,"options":[{"description":"Matches claude-code, which skips isSidechain lines. The parent's `task` tool part already stores the child's final output, so nothing user-visible is lost — only the subagent's internal steps.","label":"Skip child sessions (Recommended)"},{"description":"Each child gets its own `opencode:<ses_...>` conversation id. Full fidelity of subagent work, but ledger volume grows a lot and the parent/child relationship isn't expressible in the current schema without a new link rel.","label":"Capture as separate conversations"},{"description":"Capture children and add a links entry relating them to the parent's tool-call even